      "heading": "I. The Japanese Kanna of 1948",
      "body": "The hand plane had been carried out of Rangoon in December 1948 by Debabrata's father, Promode Ranjan Sen, wrapped inside an oiled flannel shirt at the bottom of a zinc seaman's trunk.\n\nIt was not a Western push-plane with cast-iron handles and lateral adjustment levers. It was a Japanese *kanna*—a rectangular block of white Japanese evergreen oak, nine inches long by three inches wide, bedded with a laminated carbon-steel iron forged by a tool-smith in Osaka before the war.\n\nTo use it, you did not push your weight forward against the timber. You knelt before the workbench, placed both palms across the wooden stock, hooked your index fingers over the heel of the block, and pulled the plane toward your chest with a smooth, continuous, exhalatory stroke that engaged the muscles of the lower back.\n\nWhen the iron was sharp—honed on a six-thousand-grit Kyoto water-stone until the bevel reflected the overhead lamp like dark silver—the tool made a sound unlike any modern electrical planer.\n\nIt did not scream. It made a dry, rhythmic, whispering sigh: *hussst, hussst, hussst*.\n\nBehind the blade, rising through the throat of the wooden block, came the shaving: a continuous ribbon of Burma teak, four thousandths of an inch thick, translucent as parchment, smelling of raw linseed oil, dry tobacco, and the resinous earth of tropical rainforests seventy years dead.\n\nDebabrata Sen had planed wood in the garage workshop behind 18 Dover Lane every Saturday morning of his adult life.\n\nHe was sixty-four years old. He had retired three years earlier from his position as chief structural draughtsman at the Kolkata Municipal Development Authority, where he had spent thirty-eight years drawing elevation cross-sections, calculating reinforced-concrete slab deflections, and correcting the sloppy trigonometry of private civil engineers who wanted to build five-storey apartment blocks on tidal marshland along the Eastern Metropolitan Bypass.\n\nHis hands were the hands of a man who had never separated intellectual design from manual execution.\n\nThe palms were broad, thick, scarred with white, hair-thin lines where Swedish chisel steel had slipped across knuckles in the winter of 1984; the thumbs were broad and spatulate, flattened by half a century of pressing brass try-squares against timber edges.\n\nAround him in the garage workshop, the tools hung on wooden cleats along walls of unplastered red brick: five Disston hand-saws with polished apple-wood handles; twenty-four mortise and paring chisels arranged by sixteenths of an inch from an eighth to two inches; three cast-iron Stanley smoothing planes from the 1930s; and forty wooden hand-screws with maple spindles that had been turned on a foot-lathe by his grandfather in Barisal before partition.\n\nThe house rose above the garage like an old, wounded flagship anchored in a narrow canal.\n\n18 Dover Lane had been built in the winter of 1926 by Promode Sen's elder brother, an advocate in the Calcutta High Court who had admired the Edwardian domestic architecture of Ballygunge: two storeys of deep red Surki brick laid in English bond, three-foot-thick load-bearing walls mortared with slaked lime and river sand, green louvered shutters (*khorkhori*) on all twenty-six windows, and an inner courtyard paved with square terracotta tiles where rainwater drained into an underground brick cistern.\n\nIn 1926, Dover Lane had been a quiet suburban avenue of sprawling gardens, raked cinder carriage drives, and flowering champak trees where horse-drawn landaus trotted toward the Ballygunge Circular Road.\n\nNow, the house was an architectural fossil surrounded by an encroaching forest of reinforced-concrete residential towers.\n\nTo the north, a ten-storey condominium named Silver Springs Manor rose eighty feet into the sky, its cantilevered concrete balconies casting a permanent, cold shadow across Debabrata's inner courtyard. To the east, a twelve-unit development called Heritage Enclave had driven forty steel foundation piles through the subsoil in 2019, cracking the terracotta water cistern and causing the northeastern corner of Debabrata's dining room to settle by two point four inches toward the municipal sewer line.\n\nEvery monsoon, the house protested its age.\n\nThe lime plaster in the southern bedroom blistered and flaked like dry skin; the brass window bolts oxidized into green verdigris; the thirty-foot teak rafters in the ceiling sagged beneath the weight of wet Mangalore tiles; and the heavy double doors of the ground-floor library swelled in the August humidity until they could not be closed without lifting the brass drop-handles with both hands and kicking the bottom rail with the heel of a shoe.\n\nDebabrata did not view these failures as defects.\n\nHe viewed them as the natural respiration of a living organism that had sheltered four generations of his bloodline through the Bengal famine of 1943, the riots of 1946, the refugee influx of 1971, and the slow, agonizing decay of Kolkata's industrial economy.\n\nWhen a door jammed, he did not replace it with an aluminium-framed flush door from a hardware shop in Gariahat. He took the heavy teak leaf off its brass pintle hinges, carried it out to the garage, examined the rub-marks with a carpenter's pencil, and planed three shavings from the bottom stile until the wood cleared the marble threshold by the thickness of a postcard.\n\n\"A house is like a marriage, Promode,\" his father had told him on this exact workbench forty years ago. \"If you throw it away every time it creaks in the rain, you will spend your life sleeping in hotel rooms.\"",
