      "id": "sec-ar-1b",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "I-B. The Science of the Breathable Wall (1926)",
      "heading": "I-B. The Science of the Breathable Wall (1926)",
      "body": "To understand why modern architects fail to preserve old Calcutta homes, one must understand the chemistry of lime-surki mortar.\n\nIn the winter of 1926, when Promode Sen's brother laid the foundations of 18 Dover Lane, Portland cement was an expensive British import used almost exclusively for dock quays, railway bridge abutments, and military fortifications.\n\nDomestic houses were built with chuna-surki—a traditional mortar composed of three organic and mineral ingredients:\n\nFirst, fat lime (chuna), produced by burning sea-shells gathered from the tidal mudflats of the Sundarbans in circular masonry kilns fueled with babul wood. The burned lime was slaked on site in large wooden vats, bubbling and hissing with violent exothermic heat, then allowed to age under four feet of water for sixty days until it formed a smooth, buttery, colloidal paste that smelled of sea salt and crushed oyster shell.\n\nSecond, surki—clean, well-burned clay bricks crushed into a fine, granular powder that passed through an eighty-mesh brass sieve. The crushed brick dust was not merely an inert aggregate; it was an active pozzolana, containing reactive aluminosilicates that chemically bonded with the calcium hydroxide of the lime to form durable calcium aluminate hydrates over decades of subterranean curing.\n\nThird, the organic additives: fermented molasses (gur), boiled extract of wild bael fruit pulp, and chopped hemp fibers from the jute mills of Rishra.\n\nThe molasses retarded the initial set, allowing the masons forty minutes to align each three-pound red brick with wooden plumb-bobs; the sticky bael mucilage formed an organic moisture barrier that resisted capillary rise; and the hemp fibers acted as microscopic tensile reinforcement that arrested shrinkage cracking.\n\nA wall built of lime and surki is fundamentally different from a modern wall built of Portland cement and concrete blocks.\n\nA modern cement wall is an impervious, rigid, brittle barrier. When moisture enters through a hairline crack, it is trapped within the concrete core, where it rusts the internal steel rebar, expands by six times its volume, and blows the concrete spalling off the face within twenty years.\n\nA lime-surki wall is a lung.\n\nIt is porous, flexible, self-healing. During the heavy monsoon depressions of July and August, when south Kolkata is submerged under twelve inches of rainwater, the three-foot-thick brick walls of 18 Dover Lane absorb forty gallons of atmospheric moisture into their microscopic pore structure like a sponge.\n\nThen, when the dry northwesterly winds arrive in November, the wall exhales.\n\nThe moisture evaporates through the lime-washed surface into the warm autumn air, carrying harmless soluble salts to the surface without spalling the brick or rotting the timber joists. If a slight settlement occurs in the soil, the lime mortar yields slightly, accommodating the structural displacement through microscopic plastic deformation rather than violent cracking.\n\n\"The modern engineer wants a building to be a fortress that fights the climate,\" Debabrata had written in his technical monograph for the municipal development authority in 2004. \"The traditional mason built a building to be an instrument that plays in harmony with the monsoon. You cannot conquer forty inches of rainfall with waterproof chemicals; you must give the water a path to enter, breathe, and depart.\"\n\nRitwik had read that monograph when he was an undergraduate at Kharagpur.\n\nHe had dismissed it then as romantic antiquarianism—the nostalgia of an aging draughtsman who refused to acknowledge the tensile superiority of pre-stressed concrete and structural steel.\n\nNow, standing in the Dover Lane hallway at thirty-four years old, Ritwik touched the cool, lime-washed surface of the entrance archway.\n\nThe wall was seventy-eight degrees Fahrenheit. In the street outside, the concrete façade of Silver Springs Manor was ninety-two degrees, radiating trapped tropical heat back into the alley like an electric oven.\n\nThe old lung was still breathing.",
