      "heading": "II. The Son Who Builds in Steel and Glass",
      "body": "On Tuesday, October 22, 2024, his son Ritwik arrived from Singapore on the midnight Singapore Airlines flight into Netaji Subhash Chandra Bose Airport.\n\nRitwik was thirty-four years old. He had completed his architectural degree at the Indian Institute of Technology in Kharagpur, won a Commonwealth scholarship to University College London, and now served as a principal design associate at an international architectural partnership in Singapore that specialized in high-density vertical urbanization across Southeast Asia.\n\nHe had not spent more than forty-eight hours at 18 Dover Lane in seven years.\n\nHe arrived at one-thirty in the morning in a radio taxi, carrying a dark green Rimowa polycarbonate roller suitcase and a leather shoulder satchel containing a 16-inch Apple MacBook Pro and two sets of architectural blueprints bound with black rubber bands.\n\nDebabrata had waited up for him in the ground-floor library, sitting under the green glass shade of an antique banker's lamp with an open volume of Percy Brown's *Indian Architecture* on his knee.\n\nWhen Ritwik walked into the hallway, the floorboards of the entrance vestibule groaned beneath his sneakers—that dry, sharp, high-pitched *krrrk* that Debabrata knew had a fundamental frequency of three hundred and seventy hertz.\n\n\"Baba,\" Ritwik said, setting his suitcase against the umbrella stand.\n\nHe was wearing slim black Japanese selvedge denim, an unstructured charcoal blazer of technical merino wool, and rimless titanium spectacles that made him look like a Scandinavian minimalist who had never touched a brick in his life. He was tall, thin, carrying his mother's sharp, aristocratic cheekbones and his father's stubborn, heavy jawline.\n\n\"Ritwik,\" Debabrata stood up.\n\nHe did not hug him. Bengali fathers and sons who have spent ten years avoiding each other's eyes do not hug at one-thirty in the morning. They examine each other's hairline; they register the new grey hairs around the temples; they note the slight stoop in the shoulders; and then they speak about luggage.\n\n\"The flight was on time?\" Debabrata asked.\n\n\"Ten minutes early,\" Ritwik said, running his hand through his short-cropped hair. \"The immigration line at Dum Dum was thirty minutes. The new terminal is efficient, but the air-conditioning smells of stale coolant.\"\n\nHe looked around the entrance hallway.\n\nHis eyes, trained in the hyper-functional, climate-controlled, sealed-envelope aesthetics of Marina Bay towers, swept across the lime-washed walls with an involuntary clinical grimace.\n\nHe saw the rising damp on the southern skirting board—the dark, dendritic watermarks where ground moisture had climbed twenty inches up the brickwork like climbing ivy. He saw the hairline crack that ran diagonally across the corniced ceiling, extending from the iron chandelier hook toward the library doorjamb. He smelled the unmistakable, heavy, humid odor of century-old lime mortar, old books, and stagnant river soil that no amount of cross-ventilation could ever entirely disperse.\n\n\"Baba,\" Ritwik said, dropping his satchel onto the mahogany hall table. \"You are still sleeping downstairs?\"\n\n\"The library is cooler in October,\" Debabrata said. \"I made up your room upstairs. I changed the sheets this morning. The ceiling fan has a new capacitor; it doesn't hum anymore.\"\n\nRitwik nodded, looking down at his leather shoes.\n\n\"Thanks, Baba,\" he said.\n\nNeither of them mentioned the blueprints in the leather satchel.\n\nThey both knew why he had come.\n\nThree weeks earlier, a prominent real estate development consortium from Alipore—Shree Kunj Infrastructure Private Limited—had sent Debabrata a formal letter of intent offering six crore rupees in cash plus two three-bedroom penthouse apartments in a proposed fifteen-storey luxury residential tower to be constructed on the plot of 18 Dover Lane.\n\nDebabrata had left the letter face-up on the dining table for two weeks without opening it.\n\nThen Ritwik had called from Singapore.\n\n\"Baba,\" Ritwik had said on the speakerphone, his voice tense, urgent, stripped of sentimentality. \"I have reviewed the developer's master plan and the floor-area ratio calculations. Dover Lane has been re-zoned for high-density residential under the 2020 municipal master plan. You are sixty-four years old. You cannot spend the next twenty years climbing ladders to patch lime mortar every time a thunderstorm hits south Kolkata. The house is structurally exhausted. The foundations are settling. Sign the power-of-attorney deed. Let them demolish it. You can take the penthouse on the fourteenth floor, with an elevator that opens into your living room, full power backup, and twenty-four-hour security.\"\n\nDebabrata had listened to his son's voice coming through the tiny speaker.\n\nHe had looked out of the window at the guava tree in the courtyard—the tree his mother had planted in 1968, whose roots had cracked the brick paving slabs but still produced three hundred sweet, pink-fleshed guavas every November.\n\n\"Come home for seven days, Ritwik,\" Debabrata had said quietly. \"We will talk about the contract face-to-face.\"\n\nNow Ritwik was here.\n\nAnd the blueprints were on the hall table.",
