      "id": "sec-ar-3b",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II-B. The Childhood Apprenticeship in the Garage (1995–2002)",
      "heading": "II-B. The Childhood Apprenticeship in the Garage (1995–2002)",
      "body": "Ritwik's childhood had been measured not in school semesters, but in the progression of woodworking tools his father permitted him to touch.\n\nAt seven years old, in 1997, he was allowed only to sweep the garage floor with a stiff broom of coconut leaf ribs, gathering the curls of teak and rosewood shavings into gunny sacks to be used as mulch for his mother's rose bushes in the courtyard.\n\nAt nine, he was taught the sharpening of scrapers.\n\nDebabrata would sit him on a low wooden stool beside a bucket of water, place a six-inch rectangle of Swedish carbon saw steel in a wooden vise, and hand him a polished steel burnisher made from an old engine valve stem:\n\n\"A scraper does not cut with an edge, Ritwik,\" his father had instructed, guiding the boy's small thumbs along the steel. \"It cuts with a hook. You draw the burnisher across the square corner at fifteen degrees until your thumb feels the microscopic burr turn over. If the burr is too coarse, it leaves scratches on the rosewood; if it is too fine, it polishes without removing the grain. Your fingers must learn to feel five ten-thousandths of an inch.\"\n\nAt twelve, Ritwik was permitted to handle the mortise chisels.\n\nHe had spent three months in the summer of 2002 practicing mortise-and-tenon joints on scrap pieces of seasoned sal wood. Every evening after dinner, his father would inspect the joints with an engineer's feeler gauge:\n\n\"Here,\" Debabrata would point with the tip of his pencil to a hairline gap where light showed between the tenon cheek and the mortise wall. \"Three hundredths of an inch open on the shoulder. If you glue this, the glue will hold for five years, then it will powder under humidity and the chair leg will wobble. The timber must fit dry so that when you pull it apart, it makes a sound like a cork coming out of a bottle of wine.\"\n\nRitwik had hated those inspections.\n\nHe had looked at his school friends from St. Xavier's who spent their summer afternoons playing video games on dial-up internet connections or attending tennis coaching at the South Club, and he had resented his father's relentless, uncompromising discipline.\n\nHe had felt that his father was trying to turn him into a carpenter—a manual laborer with wood dust in his hair and dirty fingernails—in a society where social status was measured by corporate air-conditioning and foreign business trips.\n\nHis mother, Manju, had understood his frustration.\n\nShe would bring out two glasses of cold lime juice with crushed mint leaves to the garage, sit on the edge of the workbench with her sketching pad, and run her soft fingers through Ritwik's hair:\n\n\"Your father doesn't want you to be a carpenter, Ritwik,\" she would say gently in the quiet of the workshop. \"He wants you to have an honest eye. An architect who doesn't know how wood meets wood is just a man who draws pictures on paper and hires poor laborers to suffer for his mistakes. If you learn how to make a joint true with your own hands, nobody will ever be able to cheat you with a shiny façade.\"\n\nShe had drawn him a small sketch on the margin of her watercolor pad: a cross-section of a traditional Japanese timber pagoda with a central independent cedar pillar that swayed during earthquakes without collapsing.\n\nBeneath the sketch, she had written four words:\n\n*Flexibility is true strength.*\n\nRitwik had kept that sketch tucked inside his passport case for twenty-two years.\n\nIt had traveled with him to Kharagpur, to London, to Singapore. Whenever an international client demanded that he cut twenty percent from the structural foundation budget to pay for expensive Italian marble cladding in a skyscraper lobby, Ritwik would look at his mother's four words and refuse to compromise.\n\nHe had become an architect of international renown because his father had forced him to feel three hundredths of an inch with his bare thumbs.\n\nAnd yet, for seven years, he had never said thank you.",
