      "id": "sec-ar-3c",
      "type": "chapter",
      "chapterTitle": "II-C. The Rangoon Timber Yards (1940–1948)",
      "heading": "II-C. The Rangoon Timber Yards (1940–1948)",
      "body": "To understand why Promode Sen had carried the Japanese plane out of Burma in 1948, one had to understand what happened to the timber yards of the Irrawaddy during the war.\n\nIn 1940, Promode had been a young timber surveyor for the Bombay Burmah Trading Corporation, stationed in the lumber docks of Ahlone on the Rangoon river. Every morning at dawn, rafts of four hundred teak logs—each trunk five feet in diameter, girdled in the Shan hills three years earlier and floated down the river on the monsoon spate—would arrive at the sawmills.\n\nPromode had learned the anatomy of teak from an old Karen master-carpenter named Saw Ba U.\n\n\"Teak is not like other trees, Promode-babu,\" Saw Ba U would say, tapping the wet bark with his wooden measuring rod. \"Other trees drink water and rot. Teak drinks silica from the mountain rock and stores natural rubber resin in its pores. When you fell an oak or a pine, the wood is dead. When you fell a Burma teak tree, the heartwood remains alive for a hundred years, resisting water, resisting fire, resisting white ants, waiting for a man who knows how to listen to the grain.\"\n\nIn March 1942, when the Japanese army entered Rangoon, the British administration ordered the scorched-earth destruction of the Ahlone timber mills.\n\nTwo million board feet of seasoned export teak were soaked in aviation fuel and set ablaze. The fire burned for fourteen days, turning the sky over the Irrawaddy into a black canopy of oily soot that blocked out the tropical sun.\n\nPromode had refused to leave his tools behind.\n\nWhile thousands of refugees fled toward India along the treacherous mountain trails of the Arakan Yoma carrying gold sovereigns and silver jewellery, Promode had packed his zinc trunk with eighteen hand planes, forty chisels, six Japanese pull-saws, and three waterstones wrapped in oilcloth.\n\nHe had walked four hundred miles on foot through the jungle, surviving on boiled rice and river water, contracting malaria twice in the mountain passes of Manipur.\n\nWhenever the refugee column paused beside a stream, Promode would open the zinc trunk, wipe each steel blade with an oily rag, and inspect the cutting edges for rust.\n\nOther refugees had laughed at him: \"Sen-babu, why are you carrying twenty pounds of iron through the jungle? You can buy chisels in Calcutta for two rupees.\"\n\nPromode had looked at the Japanese *kanna* in his hands.\n\n\"You can buy cheap English stamped steel in Calcutta,\" Promode had replied, his voice hoarse with fever. \"You cannot buy an edge that was folded sixty times by a master smith who quenched the carbon blade in the cold water of the Yodo river. In ten years, the war will be forgotten and the politicians will divide Bengal. But a man with sharp tools can always build a door that opens.\"\n\nHe had reached Calcutta in October 1948. He had walked through the refugee camps of Sealdah, found his brother's house on Dover Lane, and set up his workbench in the rear garage the following morning.\n\nFor fifty years, that zinc trunk had stood beneath the workbench—the physical vessel that had carried the integrity of craftsmanship across an empire's collapse.\n\nDebabrata had touched that trunk every morning of his life.\n\nAnd now, as he watched his son Ritwik plane the teak rail of the library door, he saw Promode's twenty-pound burden finally reach its destination: not in an Alipore real estate consortium's bank account, but in the living hands of his own grandson.",
