      "heading": "VI. The Sound of the Floorboard",
      "body": "On Friday afternoon, father and son repaired the cedar chest.\n\nThey did not use modern synthetic adhesives. They carved four butterfly keys—*chigiri*—from seasoned Indian rosewood, shaping each double-dovetail with hand chisels until the wedges fitted into the mortises across the split with an interference fit of half a millimeter.\n\nWhen the keys were tapped home with a wooden mallet and planed flush with the Japanese *kanna*, the split was permanently arrested. The crack remained visible—a dark, lightning-bolt scar across the heart of the cedar—but the wood was structurally unified, stronger at the fracture than it had been before the break.\n\nThen they re-hung the teak library door.\n\nThe two men carried the ninety-pound leaf into the house, lifted it onto the bronze pintles, and slipped the hinge pins home.\n\nDebabrata stepped back.\n\n\"Try it, Ritwik,\" he said.\n\nRitwik took the brass drop-handle in his hand.\n\nHe pushed the door shut.\n\nThe bottom rail cleared the marble threshold by two millimeters. The brass latch struck the strike-plate with a clean, deep, pneumatic *clack* that sounded like a bank vault closing in an old European financial house.\n\nThere was no rub. There was no stick. The door operated with frictionless, hydraulic grace.\n\nThat evening, Ritwik climbed the stairs to his childhood bedroom on the second floor.\n\nOn the threshold of the room, he paused before the loose teak floorboard—the board that had creaked in the key of F-sharp whenever he had tried to sneak out of the house at midnight during his college years to meet his friends at the dhaba on Southern Avenue.\n\nHe stepped on the board with his full weight.\n\nIt did not creak.\n\nHe stepped on it again. Nothing. The timber was completely silent.\n\nHe knelt down with his flashlight.\n\nBeneath the edge of the floorboard, he saw a small wedge of seasoned Burma teak—a newly planed shim, three inches long, secured by two brass screws driven clean and flush into the joist.\n\nHis father had fixed it.\n\nNot today, but years ago—perhaps the week after Ritwik had left for London—silencing the note that had announced his presence, waiting in the empty house for the day his son would return to test the wood.\n\nRitwik sat on the edge of his childhood bed.\n\nHe opened his laptop. He opened the development contract from Shree Kunj Infrastructure Private Limited.\n\nHe selected the file, dragged it into the trash icon on his desktop, and emptied the bin.\n\nHe opened a new blank file in his CAD drafting program.\n\nHe titled it: *18 Dover Lane — Structural Conservation, Light-Well Insertion, and Seismic Stabilization — Phase I.*\n\nHe worked until four in the morning, listening to the night wind rustle the leaves of the guava tree in the courtyard, watching the moonlight fall through the green louvered shutters onto the old floorboards that held him without a sound.",
