      "heading": "VII. The Courtyard Under the Guava Tree",
      "body": "On Sunday morning, before his taxi arrived to take him back to Dum Dum airport for the Singapore flight, Ritwik walked out to the garage workshop.\n\nHis father was standing at the workbench, brushing the fine teak shavings from the sole of the Japanese kanna with a horsehair bench brush.\n\nRitwik laid his roll of new architectural drawings on the bench.\n\n\"Baba,\" Ritwik said.\n\nDebabrata looked up.\n\n\"I tore up the developer's agreement,\" Ritwik said, unrolling the tracing paper. \"We are not selling the plot. But we are not leaving the house to rot either. Look here.\"\n\nHe pointed to his new cross-section:\n\n\"We will underpin the northeastern foundation with six micro-piles to stop the settlement from the high-rise next door. We will cut a light-well through the center ceiling, exactly as Ma drew in 1984, bringing natural winter sunlight into the library and drying out the rising damp. And on the eastern verandah, we will install a hydraulic platform lift that blends with the lime plaster, so that when you are seventy-five, you won't have to climb the stairs with your knees.\"\n\nDebabrata looked at the drawings.\n\nHe saw his wife's delicate architectural lines married to his son's sophisticated modern engineering. He saw eighty years of family continuity preserved not by stubborn refusal to change, but by the intelligent, loving craft of modern repair.\n\nHe reached out his broad, calloused right hand.\n\nHe did not touch the paper. He placed his hand over his son's forearm, his fingers closing around the linen sleeve with a firm, steady, immovable grip.\n\n\"How much will the micro-piles cost, Ritwik?\" Debabrata asked softly.\n\n\"About twelve lakhs,\" Ritwik smiled. \"I will pay for it from my bonus next month. Consider it my rent for the library door.\"\n\nDebabrata looked at him.\n\nFor the first time in ten years, a smile broke across his weathered face—a smile that smoothed the deep creases around his mouth and made him look like the thirty-year-old draughtsman who had built the workbench with his father in 1984.\n\n\"Twelve lakhs is too much for a door, Ritwik,\" Debabrata said, picking up the kanna. \"But for a house... for a house that can hold three generations, it is very reasonable.\"",
