      "chapterTitle": "VII-B. The Guava Tree and the Alluvial Soil",
      "heading": "VII-B. The Guava Tree and the Alluvial Soil",
      "body": "On Saturday afternoon, Debabrata took Ritwik out to the inner courtyard to examine the foundation soil.\n\nThe courtyard was twenty feet square, paved with weathered terracotta tiles that had grown a velvety patina of green moss along the mortar joints. In the center stood the guava tree, its trunk twisted and gnarled like an old rope, its bark peeling in pale cream and grey scrolls to reveal the smooth, greenish-yellow wood beneath.\n\nDebabrata knelt beside the brick cistern in the corner.\n\nHe took a steel trowel and dug through the gravel mulch until he reached the native subsoil at a depth of eighteen inches.\n\nHe scooped up a handful of dark, moist earth and placed it in Ritwik's palm.\n\n\"Feel this, Ritwik,\" Debabrata said.\n\nThe soil was cool, silky, slightly sticky between the fingers, smelling of decayed river vegetation, fresh humus, and ancient river water.\n\n\"Gangetic alluvium,\" Ritwik said, rubbing the soil between his thumb and fingers. \"High clay fraction with thirty percent fine silt. Cohesive under moderate moisture, but prone to volumetric shrinkage when the water table drops.\"\n\n\"Exactly,\" Debabrata said. \"And why did the British build two-storey brick houses on this soil without piling?\"\n\nRitwik looked at the three-foot-thick brick walls rising around the courtyard.\n\n\"Because the footings are spread footings,\" Ritwik realized, the physical logic of the architecture clicking into focus. \"The masonry steps outward by four inches every course below the plinth, creating a stepped pyramid of brick that distributes the house's dead load to less than eight hundred pounds per square foot. The house floats on the clay like a barge.\"\n\n\"And look at the tree,\" Debabrata pointed to the roots of the guava tree.\n\nThe roots spread out horizontally beneath the terracotta paving, weaving between the stepped brick footings like living cables of tendon and muscle.\n\n\"When your mother planted this tree in 1968,\" Debabrata explained, \"she planted it forty inches from the cistern. For fifty-six years, the tree has extracted forty gallons of groundwater every week during the post-monsoon drying period. It regulates the soil moisture beneath the eastern footing. If you cut down this tree, the soil beneath the dining room will experience moisture rebound, heave upward by two inches, and crack the second-floor lintels within eighteen months.\"\n\nRitwik stared at the gnarled trunk of the guava tree.\n\nHe had walked past that tree ten thousand times during his childhood. He had climbed its branches to steal guavas; he had tied his bicycle to its trunk; he had complained about the birds dropping fruit on the paving slabs.\n\nHe had never understood that the tree was a structural component of the house—a biological moisture-pump engineered by his mother's intuitive understanding of domestic ecology to stabilize the foundations.\n\nHe closed his hand around the cool Gangetic soil.\n\n\"We won't touch the tree, Baba,\" Ritwik said. \"When we install the micro-piles, we will use a rotary drill with water-injection to avoid disturbing the root zone. The tree stays.\"\n\nDebabrata stood up from the gravel.\n\nHe wiped his hands on his trousers, looked up at the green foliage where three green parakeets were eating the ripe fruit in the afternoon sun, and took a long, clean breath of autumn air.\n\n\"The tree stays,\" Debabrata agreed. \"And next month, when the guavas turn pink, I will send a carton to your office in Singapore. Let your high-rise partners taste what grows from a foundation that breathes.\"",
