      "chapterTitle": "VI-C. The Lanterns on the Eastern Verandah",
      "heading": "VI-C. The Lanterns on the Eastern Verandah",
      "body": "On Saturday evening, after the cedar chest was finished and the tools were wiped with camellia oil and returned to their wall cleats, father and son sat on the eastern verandah of Dover Lane.\n\nDebabrata had lit two brass hurricane lanterns—heavy Dietz lanterns manufactured in the 1960s, their glass globes clean and clear, casting a warm, golden circle of light across the red terracotta tiles.\n\nThe night was cool. The autumn wind was blowing from the north, rustling the green leaves of the guava tree and bringing the faint, distant sound of evening prayers from the Kali temple on Southern Avenue.\n\nIn the shadows beyond the boundary wall, the concrete cliff of Silver Springs Manor rose into the night, its fifty balconies illuminated by cold blue LED lights, its air-conditioners humming with a steady, mechanical drone that sounded like an ocean liner idling in harbor.\n\nRitwik leaned back against the lime-plastered wall.\n\nHe was holding a porcelain mug of hot ginger tea in his hands. His fingers were stiff, slightly bruised at the base of the thumb where the wooden mallet had rested, but his mind had never felt clearer.\n\n\"Baba,\" Ritwik said, watching the yellow flame flicker inside the hurricane lantern. \"When I was in London, my studio professor used to tell us that every building is a political statement. He said a building either celebrates the power of the client who paid for it, or it protests against the society that built it.\"\n\nDebabrata took a slow sip of tea.\n\n\"Your professor was a Westerner, Ritwik,\" Debabrata said gently. \"In the West, they think buildings are arguments. They build glass towers to argue with the sky, and they build concrete monuments to argue with time.\"\n\nHe looked at the open door of the library—the heavy teak leaf that now swung with the silent, effortless precision of a pendulum.\n\n\"In India,\" Debabrata said, \"a house is not an argument. A house is a hospitality. It is a place where you welcome the rain, you welcome the dust, you welcome the birds, and you welcome the children who left you and have finally found their way back. If you build a house that argues with the world, the world will tear it down in thirty years. If you build a house that forgives the world, it will stand until the bricks turn back to clay.\"\n\nRitwik closed his eyes.\n\nHe felt the cool lime plaster behind his neck; he smelled the sweet, dry fragrance of the rosewood shavings still clinging to his shirt; he heard the night wind in the guava branches.\n\nFor the first time in his life, he did not feel the need to design anything.\n\nHe was not in Singapore. He was not in London.\n\nHe was at 18 Dover Lane, sitting beside his father on an unpainted verandah, holding a cup of hot tea in the dark, completely at peace with the timber that held him.",
