      "id": "sec-ar-14",
      "type": "reflection",
      "body": "To repair something is an act of radical humility. It begins with the admission that what is broken was once whole, that it possesses an intrinsic dignity worthy of our labor, and that our own clumsy hands are capable of restoring its function. When we discard the old because it creaks, we discard the only evidence we possess that human life is capable of enduring across time. Forgiveness between a father and a son is not a legal settlement; it is a rosewood butterfly key driven across a fracture, holding two lives together until the timber forgets where it was torn."
